## Debug Printing Enums

`derive[Debug]` lets you inspect enum values during development. It prints the variant name and any data it carries.

### Basic Debug Output

```xiom
pub type Status = enum {
  Idle,
  Running,
  Stopped,
} derive[Debug]

let s = Status.Running;
io.debug(s);  // Status.Running
```

### Debug with Data

```xiom
pub type Shape = enum {
  Circle(radius: Float64),
  Rect(w: Float64, h: Float64),
} derive[Debug]

let c = Shape.Circle(5.0);
let r = Shape.Rect(10.0, 3.0);
io.debug(c);  // Shape.Circle(5.0)
io.debug(r);  // Shape.Rect(10.0, 3.0)
```

### Debug with Named Fields

```xiom
pub type Event = enum {
  Click(x: Int, y: Int),
  KeyPress(key: Str),
} derive[Debug]

let ev = Event.Click(100, 200);
io.debug(ev);  // Event.Click(x: 100, y: 200)
```

### Debugging in Practice

```xiom
pub type ApiResponse = enum {
  Success(data: Str),
  Error(code: Int),
} derive[Debug]

fn handle_response(resp: &ApiResponse) -> Unit {
  io.debug(resp);  // print what we got before processing
  // ... handle response
}
```

### Requirement Chain

All variant data types must also implement Debug:

```xiom
// Str and Int already implement Debug, so this works
pub type Msg = enum { Text(Str), Count(Int); } derive[Debug]
```

